--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Interoperability and non-affiliation statement
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ripcord is an independent interoperable client. It is NOT affiliated with,
authorised by, endorsed by, or sponsored by Sony Interactive Entertainment Inc.
or any of its affiliates.

"PlayStation", "PS5", "PS4", "DualSense", "DualShock" and "Remote Play" are
trademarks or registered trademarks of Sony Interactive Entertainment Inc.
Ripcord uses these names only where necessary to describe compatibility
truthfully (nominative use). No Sony trademark, logo, or other brand asset is
included in this repository or in any build produced from it.

Ripcord's protocol specification was derived independently, by static analysis
of the publicly distributed vendor client running on hardware the authors own,
and by capturing the authors' own network traffic between their own client and
their own console under their own account. It reproduces interface facts
required for interoperability -- protocol field numbers, byte offsets, enum
values, JSON keys and HTTP header names -- and does not reproduce vendor source
code or other authored expression. Vendor code is referenced only by relative
virtual address.

Interoperability constants
--------------------------

Ripcord includes a small set of constants (approximately 4 KB of constant
data, stored hex-encoded in an 8.7 KB JSON file: four control
key-derivation tables — a PlayStation 5 pair and a PlayStation 4 pair — four field
context keys (the registration context key is one of those four, stored a second
time under its own name), two registration key tables — a PlayStation 5 and a PlayStation 4 —
two material-transform tables likewise, and a byte offset) that the console itself
computes against. A client cannot speak this protocol without them, and they
cannot be altered without breaking interoperability, so they are interface facts
rather than authored expression.
They are included as data, read at runtime through the same configuration seam
that accepts a local override, and are not compiled into program logic.

One further constant of the same kind is compiled in rather than bundled: the
32-byte Client-Type value the console parses by content, which a message builder
needs inline. It meets the same test -- generic to the application, identical for
every client, tied to no account or console.

These values are generic to the protocol. They are identical for every user and
every console. Ripcord includes NO key material, credential, or identifier tied
to any particular console, user, or account: no registration key, no pairing
record, no session key, no device or account identifier. Those are generated or
captured per user and stay on the user's own machine.

A build that omits the constants entirely can be produced with
-p:BundleInteropConstants=false; the result is inert and reports the constants as
unavailable rather than failing at run time.

Application OAuth credential
----------------------------

Ripcord also includes the OAuth 2.0 client_id and client_secret with which the
vendor's own desktop Remote Play client authenticates to PlayStation Network.
This is stated plainly because it is a different kind of thing from the
interoperability constants above, and the two should not be read as one argument.

Those constants are values the console computes against: a client cannot speak
the protocol without them. This is an access credential for a network service,
and a client can speak the protocol without it -- a local-network session works
against a console with no internet connection at all. It is included for a
narrower reason: Sony operates no third-party client registration for this
service, so there is no credential an independent client could obtain instead.
Without it, the account-facing features -- signing in, reading the account's own
console list, and waking a console remotely -- are unreachable by any client that
is not Sony's, which would foreclose interoperability on that tier entirely.

The credential was recovered from the authors' own capture of their own network
traffic, from their own machine and their own account, which is the same source
as every other interoperability fact in this project. It was separately confirmed
to be already published and in circulation; that confirmation is a fact about the
world, established after the value was already in hand, and is not the source of
the value or the basis for including it.

The credential is generic. It is identical for every user and every console, and
authenticates an application rather than a person. Ripcord includes NO user
credential of any kind: the signed-in account's access and refresh tokens are
obtained per user at sign-in and stored encrypted on that user's own machine
(DPAPI on Windows), never in this repository or any build produced from it.

A build that omits the credential can be produced with -p:BundleOAuthClient=false;
the result reports account sign-in as unavailable and local-network play is
unaffected. At run time the bundled value is the last resort, and is overridden by
the RIPCORD_CLIENT_ID / RIPCORD_CLIENT_SECRET environment variables or by a
client.json in the user's configuration directory, so an operator with their own
registered client can substitute it without rebuilding.

Sony Interactive Entertainment may rotate this credential at any time, which would
disable Ripcord's account features until it is updated. No warranty of continued
function is offered or implied.

No vendor source code is included in any form. See docs/protocol/README.md for
the full provenance writeup.

