Third-party components
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Nothing below is vendored into this repository. The managed build resolves its
dependencies through NuGet. The console ports fetch theirs at build time, check
them against a pinned SHA-256 and compile them from source into a gitignored
directory, so a clone contains none of them. What follows is an inventory, not a
licence grant: each component is governed by its own licence and carries its own
notices.

Managed build (Windows)

  NuGet packages resolved at build time, and Microsoft platform components: the
  Windows App SDK, Media Foundation, Direct3D 12, WASAPI and GameInput.

Console ports, fetched and built from source

  openh264 2.6.0    BSD-2-Clause   ports/ripcord-ps3/tools/build-openh264.sh
  Opus 1.5.2        BSD-3-Clause   ports/ripcord-ps3/tools/build-opus.sh
  Mbed TLS 2.28.8   Apache-2.0     ports/common/tools/build-mbedtls.sh
                    (elected - see below)

  Each script pins a version and a digest and refuses a mismatch. ripcord-ps3
  builds all three; ripcord-3ds links devkitPro's builds of Opus and the same
  Mbed TLS version; the host test suite builds Mbed TLS for the machine running
  it. ports/ripcord-ps3/DECODE.md records why a permissive-only line was held
  for these and why an LGPL decoder was declined.

  MBED TLS IS DUAL-LICENSED and the table above states a choice, so the choice
  is recorded here. Upstream offers Apache-2.0 or GPL-2.0-or-later at the option
  of the user of the library. RIPCORD ELECTS THE APACHE-2.0 ARM, for the same
  reason the FreeType election below is recorded rather than left implicit: the
  arms are not interchangeable for this project, since Apache-2.0 is what
  Ripcord is distributed under and GPLv2 is not compatible with it.

  Upstream's licence file carries both arms in full, so roughly 250 lines of the
  GNU General Public License travel inside every ripcord-ps3 package. That is
  upstream's own text reproduced unmodified, not a licence this package uses,
  and the generated notices now say so at the head of that section - because a
  GPL text under an Apache heading with nothing joining the two invites exactly
  the wrong conclusion.

  openh264 and Opus are single-licence - checked against their own LICENSE and
  COPYING files rather than assumed - so Mbed TLS and FreeType are the only two
  components this project elects anything for.

Console toolchain libraries, statically linked

  ripcord-ps3 links FreeType and zlib from ps3dev's portlibs; ripcord-3ds links
  devkitPro's libctru and its builds of Opus and Mbed TLS. These are supplied by
  the installed toolchain rather than fetched by this repository, so no version
  or digest is pinned here and what a build picks up depends on the toolchain
  installed. They are third-party code in a built binary exactly as the fetched
  components above are. Their licences are the upstream projects' own, and this
  project has not separately catalogued them - work that belongs with a binary
  release rather than with the source.

  FreeType is offered under either the FreeType License or GPLv2, at the choice
  of the user of the library. RIPCORD ELECTS THE FREETYPE LICENSE. The election
  is recorded here rather than left implicit because the two arms are not
  interchangeable for this project: the FTL is permissive, while GPLv2 and the
  Apache-2.0 licence this project is distributed under are not compatible. A
  build that drifted into the GPLv2 arm by nobody choosing would be a licensing
  problem discovered late.

  THE FTL ASKS FOR TWO DIFFERENT THINGS AND A BINARY TRIGGERS BOTH. Section 2
  asks for a credit; section 1 separately requires, of redistribution in binary
  form, "a disclaimer that states that the software is based in part of the work
  of the FreeType Team, in the distribution documentation". Only the credit was
  recorded here until 2026-09-18, and it carried no year:

      Portions of this software are copyright (c) 2026 The FreeType Project
      (https://freetype.org). All rights reserved.

      This software is based in part on the work of the FreeType Team.

  Both lines ship inside every ripcord-ps3 package, in USRDIR/THIRD-PARTY-
  NOTICES.txt, because "the distribution documentation" for someone who
  downloads a .pkg is what is inside the .pkg - not a file in this repository
  that they never receive. The disclaimer is emitted unconditionally by the
  generator, so it cannot be lost to a missing file the way a copied licence
  text can.

  FTL section 3 also forbids either party using the other's name for
  commercial, advertising or promotional purposes without written permission.
  Nothing in this project does; recorded so it stays a decision.

Console firmware, used at runtime and never redistributed

  The ports reach their console's own system modules through the toolchain's
  stub libraries: on PS3 the video decoder, RSX, audio, networking, filesystem
  and system-utility modules. ripcord-ps3 also reads the system typeface from
  /dev_flash at runtime and rasterises it with FreeType, which is what the
  firmware's own font API would have done with the same file; the typeface stays
  on the console. No firmware component - no module, no font, no data file - is
  copied into this repository or into a built binary.

A DISTRIBUTED BINARY IS NOT THIS REPOSITORY, and ripcord-ps3 now produces one.
A built .self, .pkg or .3dsx links the components above statically, so whoever
publishes one is distributing them and takes on the attribution and notice
obligations that come with each.

This section is the inventory. The notices themselves are generated at package
time by ports/ripcord-ps3/tools/gen-third-party-notices.sh, from the licence
texts shipped with the exact versions linked into that build, and land in the
package at USRDIR/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt. Generated rather than committed for
the same reason the interop constants are: a checked-in copy of somebody else's
licence is a second copy that drifts, and the one that matters is the licence of
the version actually linked.

The generator exits non-zero if a licence that REQUIRES reproduction is missing
- openh264, Opus and Mbed TLS all do - and the package will not build without
them. Shipping a binary with an incomplete notices file is worse than not
shipping one.

ripcord-3ds does not yet have the equivalent, and no .3dsx is released.
